diagnosis_text:
I focused on Location C’s gateway segment 192.168.200.0/24 (router4 eth0=192.168.200.1 via switch4 → switch5/6 → PCs). I ran parallel, sustained pings from pc11–pc14 to 192.168.200.1 and return pings from router4 to each PC; all showed 0% loss during test windows, with 1–4 ms RTTs. Router4’s eth0 (192.168.200.1) and switch4’s uplink to it show no link flaps (carrier_changes=2 and stable on both ends), no errors, no drops (tc fq_codel: 0 drops), and ethtool reports full‑duplex link up. ARP is steady: all PCs resolve 192.168.200.1 to ae:d2:4b:54:82:90 (router4’s MAC), and router4 sees the four PC MACs on eth0 in REACHABLE/STALE as expected. The layer‑2 bridges (switch4/5/6) have consistent FDB entries pointing the router MAC toward switch4, and the PC MACs toward their correct access ports; no STP events (STP disabled) or MAC flapping observed. Router4 has no firewall rules blocking ICMP (iptables ACCEPT), and ICMP rate limiting is disabled (net.ipv4.icmp_ratelimit=0).

Mechanistically, if the gateway were hiccuping, we would expect packet loss, ARP churn, link flaps, queue drops, or FDB instability—none are present. Localization therefore does not indicate a device/interface fault on router4 or the L2 path.

Root cause: No fault was reproducible or evidenced on the gateway or switching path during testing.

Corrective fix: No changes applied. To catch intermittent issues, deploy continuous pings/smokeping from one PC to 192.168.200.1 and enable STP on switches as a safeguard against accidental loops. If hiccups recur, capture concurrent “ip -s link”, “bridge fdb”, and dmesg on switch4 and router4 for correlation. === END ===